<!--
================================================================================
 home.srv  --  the homepage (a plain page body)
================================================================================
 A plain .srv body is pasted, unchanged, into the #middle element of every
 page. It may contain any HTML/CSS/JS. The page list and nav tabs come from
 srvsite.toml; this file is only the body of the homepage.

 It contains two forms:
   * a "client key" login form (POSTs {"chat_0_login":"<key>"} to /form)
   * a generic contact form (POSTs {"srvsite_contact":"<json>"} to /form)
================================================================================
-->

<style>
	#home-hero {
		background-color: var(--ink-panel);
		border-radius: 10px;
		box-shadow: 0 10px 28px rgba(0,0,0,.45);
		padding: 2em 1.5em;
		margin: 1.5em 1em;
		color: var(--ink);
		line-height:1.5em;
	}
	#home-hero h2 { margin-top: 0; }
	#login-panel {
		background-color: var(--brown-deep);
		border-radius: 10px;
		padding: 1.25em 1.5em;
		margin: 1.5em 1em;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	#contact-panel {
		background-color: var(--ink-panel);
		border-radius: 10px;
		box-shadow: 0 10px 28px rgba(0,0,0,.45);
		padding: 1.5em;
		margin: 1.5em 1em;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	.pj-input {
		padding:.1em .4em;
		border:none;
		font-size: 1em;
		color:black;
		margin:.4em .2em;
		background-color: var(--top-white);
		height:1.5em;
		border-radius:.4em;
		outline: none;
	}
	.pj-submit {
		border:none;
		background-color:var(--sage);
		padding:5px 15px;
		margin:0;
		border-radius:20px;
		font-size:.9em;
		color:black;
		font-weight:bold;
		cursor: pointer;
	}
	#contact-panel textarea {
		display:block;
		width: 100%;
		min-height: 90px;
		margin: .4em 0;
		padding: .4em;
		border: none;
		border-radius: .4em;
		font-family: inherit;
		font-size: 1em;
		background-color: var(--top-white);
		color: black;
		box-sizing: border-box;
	}
</style>

<div id="home-hero">
	<h2>SRVsite &middot; a tiny, database-free website engine</h2>
	<p style="color: var(--dim);">
		<small><strong>From the author:</strong></small>

		<em>
		<br /><br />Tonight I'm open-sourcing SRVsite. Here's a <a href="https://srvpress.com/srvtest/">very basic demo</a> of 
		the open source version of SRVsite. And here's the Github repository: <a href="https://github.com/jaybrunet/srvsite">https://github.com/jaybrunet/srvsite</a>

		<br /><br />I started on the original idea around 2007, because I was frustrated with the speed and
		complexity of WordPress. Originally this was a single HTML/Javscript file that rendered all the pages. The SPA 
		(Single-Page Application) paradigm lasted a few years, until everyone realized it didn't scale well, and
		arguably it wasn't very SEO friendly. Still I wanted a simple website that rendered instantly without
		a database. Simplicity scales, complexity fails!

		<br /><br />I never really wanted to be a database administrator, but as a WordPress system admin, that's just
		part of the job description now. With SRVsite there's no database to install, or import, or export, and no 
		InnoDB RAM to allocate. It's just easier to manage year after year.
		
		<br /><br />Still hooked on the basic idea, I started over again using <a href="https://nodejs.org">Node</a>. 
		But by 2020 I found the event-driven nature of Javascript was too unpredictable and clumsy. 
		Eventually I started using Go, a straighforward language that runs everything in order sequentially. 
		SRVsite was born, the software underlying the <a href="https://srvpress.com">SRVpress</a> website.

		<br /><br />The beauty of Go, it uses very little memory compared to PHP, and you don't need many dependencies, 
		and there are few moving parts compared to the old WordPress LEMP stack. Working with WordPress intimately for decades 
		professionally as a blogger, as a web developer, and as a system admin, the pain is always in the complexity, 
		especially when it comes to migrations. Nobody likes migrations, except maybe sales people!

		<br /><br />Recently, <a href="https://frankenphp.dev/">FrankenPHP</a> (also written in Go) reduced the
		stack complexity for WordPress admins, completely eliminating the need for PHP-FPM. Yet the WordPress codebase is 
		still enormous, unwieldy and difficult to build on. My original intention was to have SRVsite run without 
		Nginx, simplifying installation further. But now I prefer using <a href="https://caddyserver.com/">Caddy</a> (instead
		of Nginx) because it handles https certificate renwals automatically. FWIW 
		<a href="https://certbot.eff.org/">Certbot</a> is fantastic, but with Caddy you don't even need to think about 
		certificates. If you need help configuring Caddy, and/or would like me to configure your SRVsite server, let me know.
		
		<br /><br />The way I designed SRVsite, you can easily edit pages "in production" without rebuilding or
		recompiling anything. See changes instantly. If you want, you *can* use a separate development environment and 
		just upload your .srv files when you're ready to deploy them, but it's not a necessity. 
		
		<br /><br />In my professional opinion, this is better than "static website" generators that require an extra "build" 
		step, and then you stuggle with nagivation. With SRVsite, basic navigation is automatic. The way I see it, static pages 
		are a big step backwards. Let's move forward. SRVsite is the best of both worlds, you get the simplicity and speed 
		of static pages, with the intelligence and extensibility of a modern programming language.

		<br /><br />And now that we have AI, SRVsite is even more attractive. Because
		the codebase is so tiny, AI agents can digest it in seconds and create just about anything you want. Building upon 
		a smaller foundation is generally smarter (fewer tokens) than refactoring a huge application like WordPress, which 
		is prone to break and burn through all your inference tokens. Let me know if you use SRVsite as a foundation for 
		building websites with AI!

		<br /><br /><a href="https://PJBrunet.com">Jay Brunet</a> -- September 28, 2026
		<br /><br />

		</em>
		
	<p>
		SRVsite is a small, self-contained website engine written in Go. It keeps
		its configuration in <strong>TOML</strong>, its users in
		<strong>users.json</strong> and its content in plain <strong>.srv</strong>
		files &mdash; no database required. Adding a page is one
		<code>[[pages]]</code> block in <code>srvsite.toml</code> plus a body file.
	</p>
	<p>
		Pages can carry any HTML, CSS and JavaScript you like; a page may also name an optional
		config file to become a data-driven page such as a blog.
	</p>
	<p class="srv-gold">
		For technical support or managed hosting, visit <a href="https://srvpress.com">SRVpress.com</a>
	</p>
</div>

<!-- ===================== client-key login ===================== -->
<div id="login-panel">
	<form id="license-login-form" style="border:0;">
		<strong class="srv-gold">Client login</strong>
		<div style="font-size:16px; margin-top:.5em;">
			<em>Client key</em>
			<input id="license-input" type="text" placeholder="DEMO0001-00000000-00000000-00000001" value="" maxlength="35" class="pj-input" />
			<input id="license-submit-input" type="button" value="Login" class="pj-submit" />
			<div style="display:none;color:#E88;">That key was not accepted.</div>
		</div>
	</form>
	<div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:.6em;">
		A valid key opens the members page (<code>/studio</code>). The demo keys
		shipped with this project are listed in <code>README.md</code>.
	</div>
</div>

<!-- ===================== contact form ===================== -->
<div id="contact-panel">
	<h3>Get in touch</h3>
	<form id="contact-form" style="border:0;">
		<input id="contact-name" type="text" placeholder="your name" class="pj-input" style="width:14em;" />
		<input id="contact-email" type="email" placeholder="you@example.com" class="pj-input" style="width:14em;" />
		<textarea id="contact-message" placeholder="how can we help?"></textarea>
		<input id="contact-submit" type="button" value="Send" class="pj-submit" />
		<span id="contact-done" style="display:none;color:var(--sage);margin-left:.6em;">Thanks, message sent.</span>
	</form>
</div>
